GitOps Workflow (End-to-End)

✨ Hello DevOps Learners!
Continuing my GitOps learning journey after completing Ansible, today I’m exploring the end-to-end GitOps workflow and how changes move from a developer to a running application in Kubernetes. 🚀
GitOps simplifies deployments by ensuring that Git controls the entire delivery process.

🔹 Step 1 — Developer Makes Changes
 A developer writes application code or updates configuration files.
 These changes may include:
Kubernetes YAML manifests
Deployment configurations
Infrastructure settings
The changes are then committed to the Git repository.

🔹 Step 2 — Changes Stored in Git
 Once the code is pushed, Git becomes the source of truth.
 Teams can review changes through pull requests and version control history before merging them.
This ensures better collaboration and traceability.

🔹 Step 3 — GitOps Tool Detects Changes
 GitOps tools continuously monitor the Git repository.
 When a new commit is detected, the tool compares the desired state in Git with the current state in the cluster.
If differences exist, the system prepares to apply updates automatically.

🔹 Step 4 — Deployment to Kubernetes
 The GitOps tool synchronizes the cluster with the Git repository.
 The Kubernetes cluster is updated so that the actual state matches the desired state defined in Git.
This creates a fully automated and reliable deployment process.

💡 Key Idea:
 The GitOps workflow ensures that every deployment begins with a Git change, making infrastructure predictable, traceable, and automated.
